# Understanding MCP based programming language
_Exported on 8/12/2025 at 01:23:34 EDT from Cursor (1.4.3)_

---

**User**

Do you understand this MCP based programming language I'm making? I'm making it in natural language. A few hints:

# implies the purpose of the program

context: implies function parameters, to be listed after a functions declaration

1., 2., ..., a, b, ..., i, ii, ..., -, imply functions, with each function outlining further specificity vertically, while also indicating piping of returns. I.e., the program is sequencial and intended to stay small. We aren't orchestrating anything here, keep it linear.

() implies a return statement, i.e., this is how we'll embed further specificity within a function, a.k.a. horizontally.

*{}* is a way to provide further context, horizontally -- e.g., something to keep in mind when implementing the solution.

TODO is where the programmer left off, and intends to continue later. Like a save point. They may also use this as a breakpoint, to test if the program is working properly. The program would exit and print a log of the program's state/findings, to support iterative programming of this protocol.

If you understand this MCP execution based programming language, please write an instruction protocol/ruleset doc(s) that you will refer to, to eventually follow. In these docs, use natural language to describe the syntax. Think of these docs as a compiler or library, i.e., a set of common instructions that you may reference at runtime, in case you get stuck or confused during the execution of a program. 

Note, these docs you will reference aim to serve as the literal framework and infrastructure of a modern programming language that specializes in the execution and piping of MCP commands. As time goes on and new syntax is required to support different/more precise execution, you'll have to expand the docs to outline the given convention.

